## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of 3-valued logic systems and constraint propagation techniques in scenarios with incomplete information.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene's 3-valued logic semantics (T>U>F ordering)
- Truth tables: ¬U=U, ∧/∨ as min/max operations
- Implication definition: A → B ≡ ¬A ∨ B
- Backtracking with unknown values and constraint propagation
- Concrete validation through systematic evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued logic evaluation helpers
const vals = {T: 2, U: 1, F: 0};
function not(x) { return x === 2 ? 0 : x === 0 ? 2 : 1; }
function and(a, b) { return Math.min(a, b); }
function or(a, b) { return Math.max(a, b); }
function implies(a, b) { return or(not(a), b); }
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with non-classical truth in a constrained system, using backtracking under Kleene semantics and additional operators (↔, ⊕) to reveal subtle propagation patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics and derived operators
- Backtracking with constraint propagation
- Handling composite operators like biconditional and XOR under 3-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function imp(a,b){ // a→b = (¬a) ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return (na==='T' || b==='T') ? 'T' : (na==='U' || b==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
}
function orb(a,b){ // max in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b;
}
function land(a,b){ // min in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b;
}
```

